./.gemini/tmp/94a6b447580330f9f2b609422537b04239ff3a39df9137e32efd559f1a2935cb/chats/session-2026-01-29T09-27-12acddf1.json:      "content": "I'll verify the availability of `com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.OptBoolean`. If it is, I'll use `@JacksonInject(useInput = OptBoolean.FALSE)` to ensure `JavaScriptConfig` can only be injected and not overridden by JSON input.",
./.gemini/tmp/94a6b447580330f9f2b609422537b04239ff3a39df9137e32efd559f1a2935cb/chats/session-2026-01-29T09-27-12acddf1.json:          "description": "I've realized that `@JacksonInject` without a name uses the parameter's type for lookup in `InjectableValues`. It's still unclear why `""` in JSON would match this. I'm speculating `""` might be treated as a missing property needing mapping. I'm revisiting `JavaScriptConfig.java`, particularly the `@JsonCreator` annotation. I'm planning a code change to test this and see how JSON property naming affects injection.",
./.gemini/tmp/94a6b447580330f9f2b609422537b04239ff3a39df9137e32efd559f1a2935cb/chats/session-2026-01-29T09-27-12acddf1.json:          "description": "I'm now focusing on a specific scenario: preventing user override of `JavaScriptConfig`. I've realized an empty string key might be the root cause in some Jackson versions. I'm considering moving the `JavaScriptConfig` parameter outside the `@JsonCreator` constructor. I'm exploring a two-step injection approach: using a constructor for JSON properties and `@JacksonInject` on a field. However, it looks like `@JacksonInject` isn't optimal on a final field. I'm also considering another constructor for Jackson.",
